    "answer": {
      "short": "Versines and haversines were used in astronomical and nautical calculations, with tables helping navigators perform calculations by hand. Calculators and computers have reduced the need for separate named functions and tables. The haversine formula still provides spherical great-circle distances, including approximate distances between geographical locations.",
      "worked": "One possible paragraph: Versine and haversine were useful in astronomical and nautical calculations before electronic calculators. Navigators used tables of these functions and their logarithms to simplify calculations involving positions and angles; the haversine is half the versine. Their separate names and tables are less necessary now because calculators and computers can evaluate equivalent expressions in sine and cosine directly. Haversine nevertheless survives in the great-circle distance formula, which can estimate the distance between two geographical locations by treating Earth as a sphere.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
